================================================================================
1. FINDING BY FINDING
================================================================================

F1 (was blocking: probe read unmapped memory, error code discarded).
  Fixed. kernel/src/proc.rs's `bad_mem_user_entry` now reads 0xffffffff80000000
  (mapped kernel text) and trustselftest.rs asserts vector 14, cr2 equal to that
  address, P=1 (code & 1) and U=1 (code & 4). The author's log carries a real
  guest line, and it agrees with the arithmetic of a user read of a supervisor
  page:

    proc: PID 2 (bad_mem) killed by fault: vector=14 code=0x5 rip=0x400007 cr2=0xffffffff80000000

  code=0x5 is 0b101: P=1, W=0, U=1 — exactly what the MMU sets for a CPL3 read
  of a present supervisor page (Intel SDM Vol 3A, "Page-Fault Error Code").
  My plant C (section 3) shows the P-bit assertion is load-bearing.

F2 (was high: ring 3 proven only by consequences).
  Fixed. idt.rs copies the hardware CS out of the trap frame: `[rsp + 40]` on
  the user-fault path and `[rsp + 120]` on syscall_entry, and proc.rs stores it
  on the Process and in ProcessExitReason::Fault. I checked both offsets against
  the frame layouts in the same file rather than taking them on trust:

  - common_exc builds a uniform frame on the handler stack and documents
    +0 vector, +8 flags, +16 code, +32 rip, +40 cs, +48 rflags, +56 rsp, +64 ss
    (idt.rs comments at the `mov [rsp + 40], rdx` copy). `mov r8, [rsp + 40]`
    therefore passes CS, and that slot was copied from the interrupted stack.
  - syscall_entry pushes 14 registers (14 × 8 = 112 bytes) before
    `mov r8, [rsp + 120]`; 112 is the user RIP slot of the hardware frame, 120
    the CS slot. Correct for a `int`-gate entrance, which is what the IDT sets.

  The author's log shows the value and its plant:
    K4_PROC_PROBE novad_syscall_frame_cs=0x3b cpl=3
    (plant, ring 0 entry) K4_PROC_PROBE novad_syscall_frame_cs=0x8 cpl=0  -> FAIL
  My plant A (section 3) reads [rsp+112] instead and turns the check red.

F3 (was high: switch_to did not reload TSS.rsp0 or switch contexts).
  Fixed. switch_to now calls gdt::set_tss_rsp0(proc.kstack_top) before the CR3
  write, and gdt.rs exposes get_tss_rsp0(). The new check switches
  novad -> bad_mem -> kernel and asserts current_pid, live CR3 (masked) and live
  TSS.rsp0 after each switch. See finding N1: it proves the field was written,
  not that a ring 3 -> ring 0 transition lands on that stack. My plant B removes
  the reload and the check goes red, so it is not decorative.

F4 (was medium: faulted/exited processes never released their slots).
  Fixed in substance. alloc_process_slot() is two-pass (Unused, then Exited plus
  cleanup_process), and the check spawns and reaps 10 processes (15 total with
  the earlier ones). The author's plant (recycling disabled) fails at iteration 2.
  See finding N2 for a real defect inside the new cleanup path.

F5 (was low: get_process_mut revived Unused slots).
  Fixed. Both accessors now test `state != Unused`, and PID 0 is initialised as
  Running (Process::new_kernel). The check walks all 8 slots and compares the two
  answers. Nothing to break here that is not covered by F4's test.

F6 (was medium: novad's CapSet came from a hardcoded array, not the manifest).
  Fixed, and better than the finding asked. At 00019f1 sched::init() calls
  trust::verify_and_authenticate_package(...) and then
  proc::spawn_verified_user(&pkg, ...), which spawns with pkg.caps
  (proc.rs:180-185) — so the set really comes from the authenticated manifest.
  eff20d9 replaced the five-substring search with an array scan of
  "capabilities": [...], and added the stale-signature test. See finding N3 for
  the one path still untested.

F7 (was medium: every capability gated only its own request).
  Fixed as documented. cap.rs gives each Resource a gating_status() and
  has_live_kernel_subsystem(); docs/architecture.md has the inventory section;
  the check pins 3 live and 5 unbuilt against CAP_REFS, which has 8 entries
  (counted in cap.rs:109-118). See finding N4 on what "live" means for FsRead.

F8 (was low: banner still said "ring 0 only, no isolation yet").
  Fixed. The banner now reads "per-process PML4, Ring 3 user mode, APIC timer
  preemption", and the run ends with a summary line, "trust gate:  45 checks
  passed, 0 failed". 45 is consistent with the data: my plant runs print
  "44 checks passed, 1 failed" for the same tree, i.e. 45 checks exist.

F9 (was medium: scheduler dead code).
  Resolved by K4-T, as A2.8 says, and I confirmed the wiring rather than the
  claim: idt.rs:440 calls crate::sched::preempt_timer_tick(saved_rsp), and that
  function does the round-robin pick, updates TSS.rsp0 and calls switch_to.
  What I did not re-run: the author's "disable APIC preemption and the spinner
  hangs" plant (that would be a fourth CI run for one claim).

F10 (was low: a second, unlogged handle_syscall in proc.rs).
  Fixed. `git grep -n "fn handle_syscall" 00019f1 -- kernel/` returns nothing;
