================================================================================
2. ONE TEST READ CLOSELY: the F1 fault check in trustselftest.rs
================================================================================

  let mem_faulted = match mem_exit {
      proc::ProcessExitReason::Fault { vector, code, cr2, .. } => {
          vector == 14
              && cr2 == 0xffff_ffff_8000_0000
              && (code & 1) != 0
              && (code & 4) != 0
      }
      _ => false,
  };

  Left side: vector, code and cr2, produced by the CPU's #PF and carried through
  idt.rs's frame into proc::handle_user_fault without being rewritten (only code's
  bit 0 is now proven load-bearing by my plant C).
  Right side: the constants.
  - 14 and the bit meanings come from the architecture, not from this kernel
    (SDM Vol 3A #PF vector and error-code layout).
  - (code & 1) and (code & 4) are facts the kernel's page tables produce; the
    probe cannot choose them. That is what makes plant C meaningful: clearing P
    in the saved code (a change on the *observation* side, not the probe side)
    fails the check.
  - cr2 == 0xffff_ffff_8000_0000 is weaker than it looks: the same literal is
    assembled into bad_mem's probe in proc.rs. If someone moved the probe and
    forgot the assertion, the test fails, so it is not a tautology — but the
    address is a constant this repository chose twice, not an external fact. The
    independent parts of this check are the P/U bits and vector 14.

