================================================================================
4. NEW FINDINGS (for the author; not fixed here)
================================================================================

N1 (medium, F3). The switch test asserts get_tss_rsp0() == proc.kstack_top,
  i.e. it reads back the word switch_to just wrote. It proves the reload happens
  and cannot drift, but it does not prove the CPU uses it: a ring 3 fault is the
  only thing that loads TSS.rsp0, and no test takes a fault *from* a user process
  after a deliberate switch and checks which stack the handler ran on. A test
  that switches to process B, faults from B, and asserts the handler's stack lies
  in B's kstack frame would close it. Low risk today because ring 0 has one
  stack, but the claim in sched.rs ("TSS.rsp0 dynamic reloading on process
  switch") is still only half proven.

N2 (medium, F4). cleanup_process frees the user stack with
  `pmm::free_frame(proc.ustack_top - 4096 - hhdm)` guarded by
  `if proc.ustack_top > hhdm`. But ustack_top is USER_STACK_TOP = 0x0080_0000, a
  *virtual* constant, and hhdm is 0xffff800000000000 (the probes in the same run
  print rsp0=0xffff80001cff2000). So the guard is always false and the branch is
  dead. It is dead luckily: the same page is already freed by
  destroy_user_space(), which walks the low half of the PML4 and frees every
  present frame, user stack included; and `0x0080_0000 - 4096 - hhdm` is not a
  physical frame at all (it is 0x0080_7ff0_0000 after wrapping, which is aligned,
  so pmm::free_frame would not reject it — it would push a garbage frame into the
  free list). Two bugs that cancel only because a comparison is always false.
  Delete the branch (the page is reclaimed) or recompute from the frame the way
  spawn_user does. Not blocking the F4 fix, which works, but it is a trap for
  whoever changes the HHDM base or reuses this code.

N3 (low, F6). The new "added capability" test is rejected with
  SignatureRejected because the signature is stale — which is correct, but it
  means the new parser's failure path (a validly signed manifest naming an
  unknown capability -> UnknownCapability) is never exercised. The parser is
  covered indirectly by the novad CapSet check (it must find exactly fs:read and
  compute:verify and nothing else), so this is a gap in coverage, not in
  behaviour.

N4 (low, F7). The inventory counts FsRead as one of the 3 "live kernel
  subsystems" while the same commit's docstring says FsRead "GATES TOKEN ONLY
  ... ring 3 user fd read syscalls are deferred to K6-0". At 00019f1 that is
  accurate for the syscall path; the number 3 is a labelling convention ("the
  kernel has code behind it") rather than a count of enforced operations. It
  becomes true as a syscall gate only with 6df6921 (K6-0). The check pins the
  convention, so nobody can quietly change the labels; the wording is what could
  mislead.

N5 (repository state, not this series). main is red on push at the moment,
  through the known K5c tone flake. My own docs-only push (6cddfff, the F2.7
  review) failed with:

    FAIL: audio too short: 1328 samples at 44100 Hz
    FAIL: SeaBIOS/selftest tone verification failed

  Same class as 6df6921 (1330) and b689faf (1770) in docs/queue-2026-11-06.md.
  It is A2.20's item; I did not touch it.

